WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.360
Film Courage: Ten years ago I believe you
visit The Cat House of the Kings

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.430
Jack Perez, Writer/Director: The Cat House
on the Kings, yes.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.120
Film Courage: Cat House on the Kings, sorry.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.700
So how did this wonderful sanctuary change
your life?

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.509
Jack: Oh it was huge!

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:14.509
You know we’ve talked about this…we had
a feral cat.

00:00:14.509 --> 00:00:18.090
We’ve always had feral cats come to our
house because we have lots of cats.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:23.119
And a feral cat (a wild cat) will sense that
cat people live there.

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:29.330
And a feral cat obviously cannot be pet or
touched or anything (they are wild animals).

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.330
But we’ve done this over the years and we’ve
fed feral cats.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:37.510
So I guess it was a little over ten years
ago we were moving from out apartment and

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:42.010
just as we were about to move a feral cat
showed up and it was like “feed me.”

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.530
And we were like “Yes, of course we’ll
feed you, but we’re moving.”

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.630
And we were faced with this prospect of like
“What are we going to do?

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.570
This animal is dependent upon us now but we’re
moving.”

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:57.710
It got to the point where I was literally
driving across town every day to leave food

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:58.830
at this empty house.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.820
This cat was standing there (it was the saddest
thing).

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:01.950
It was like “Where’s my food?”

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.030
I’m driving across town, I can’t keep
this up.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.490
I can’t expect the new tenants to take care
of him or whatever.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:16.210
So I started looking into resumes and looking
into that whole world and I became educated.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.210
I was like “Wow?

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.390
There are just tons of kill shelters.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.350
If you bring an animal to a regular shelter,
they will be killed.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.780
Certainly a feral can never be adopted (rarely).

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.370
What do you do with a feral cat?

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.350
And so after a lot of research I found this
one place which I had never heard of The Cat

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:37.610
House on the Kings which was this one woman’s
massive property up near Fresno which she

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:43.500
had turned into this ranch style multi acre
property into this sanctuary and adoption

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.730
center where she had almost a thousand animals
that were totally cared for (it wasn’t a

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.210
hoarder) it was like an operation.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:59.920
And she was like “Yeah, we actually have
a feral section and if you can catch him,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.380
you can bring him here.”

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.600
And everybody else was like “We’re full
up and they’ll kill them.”

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:06.780
And this was the one place.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.530
And when I went up there it was like one woman
and one other person (or a couple) and all

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.569
of these animals and I was like the definition
to me of like a saint.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:21.770
This was like this woman who had dedicated
her whole life to these animals (literally)

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:24.420
and it was like making it work.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.640
She definitely needed help.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.890
And I said to myself “My God, what can I
do in exchange for this?

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:36.060
You saved this cat’s life and these thousands
of other dogs and cats.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.849
So I decided to make this Youtube video.

00:02:38.849 --> 00:02:43.450
I said “I’ll come back and do a portrait
of you and the place, a couple of minutes

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:44.890
and we’ll put it on Youtube.”

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:51.770
And this was when Youtube was sort of new
and just as when luck would have it there

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:57.319
were enough cat people out there that it started
to go viral and people really saw that this

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:02.480
was this beautiful soul that was doing something
so unique and she started to get donations

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.739
and she started to get more well known.

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:12.980
And now (10-13 years later) it’s considered…it’s
a very well-known institution around the world.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:17.349
People come from all over the world to visit
this place and support it.

00:03:17.349 --> 00:03:21.330
So it’s a big part of SEARCH &amp; RESCUE’s
story.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:26.030
The ending of the whole movie actually happens
at the Cat House, actually takes place at

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.030
the Cat House.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:32.709
And I’m trying to talk Lynea Lattanzio (the
proprietor) into actually playing herself

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:37.230
in the movie if she’ll agree to do it because
she’s such a character.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.950
But yes the whole movie actually resolves
at The Cat House.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:45.510
Film Courage: Had you thought about what would
have happened if you had moved one week earlier

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.510
and never met that one cat?

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.800
Jack: I hadn’t really thought about that?

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.940
It’s been such a…my wife has said “It’s
the most important film you’ve ever made

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:59.060
was that film for The Cat House because (talking
about direct action) that film because it

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:07.420
went viral, because I made it, because it
exists and it reached people, that institution,

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:13.670
that sanctuary received much-needed donations
that saved a lot more animals.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:21.620
And so in terms of doing something that is
valuable (truly valuable) that little film

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:26.279
when I think about it was that little film
is definitely up there in being an important

00:04:26.279 --> 00:04:30.830
movie of mine because it helped, it ultimately
helped a lot of animals.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.400
Film Courage: Do you know how many views that
video has?

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:42.770
Jack: Well I think know it’s well over a
million views.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:49.870
But conversely I did this fun monster thing
for Youtube years ago (Fear Force 5), that

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.849
has 50 million views now.

00:04:52.849 --> 00:04:57.150
But the million and a half or whatever The
Cat House has is way more valuable because

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:02.129
that just means something in real world terms.

00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:08.059
There were a lot of animals that got saved
because people gave because they saw the video.

00:05:08.059 --> 00:05:10.439
So I don’t know what would have happened.

00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:15.990
Certainly SEARCH &amp; RESCUE wouldn’t have
evolved the way that it did because there

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:25.379
is a cat component, a feral component and
a rescue component to the story that is a

00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:30.860
thread in this crime story that is a direct
result of my experience with The Cat House

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.559
on the Kings and why I feel it’s appropriate
to end the movie there even though there are

00:05:34.559 --> 00:05:38.940
gangsters and assassins and peddlers
and all this other stuff in the movie, it

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:43.729
ultimately ends at The Cat House which is
like the sweetest place on earth.

00:05:43.729 --> 00:05:44.229
Check out Jack’s video on The Cat House
on the Kings (link in description)

